PRODUCT MODEL DECIDED (Peter 2026-08-10) — supersedes deck-as-destination + the chat models. LINEAGE CORRECTED: (a) forkchat/strandchat = WRONG MODEL (chat-shaped thinking never fit driving parallel strands). (b) the tmux deck = a PROTOTYPE, not the destination — cheapest way to FEEL the pane-of-glass with real sessions; it proved the concept and its limits (jumps, can't share screen with a browser, pane-can't-leave) were the prototype doing its job. (c) THE real undertaking = TERMS IN A BROWSER (the browser-compositor pivot is now THE PLAN, not a candidate). LAYOUT MODEL — reversed from the tmux deck: (1) main working area EXPANDS DOWNWARD, not sideways — a tall vertical SCROLL, not a wide grid. This IS the 'no jump' answer: vertical scroll is continuous + natural (every web page/chat already works this way; the eye tracks it) where sideways paging JUMPED. (2) THUMBNAIL STRIP on the RIGHT = the navigator: small LIVE previews of each term (browser tab-preview column / slide-sorter); glance right for all-strands state, click a thumbnail to bring it into the main area. Replaces BOTH the overview AND get-lost-between-pages — always-visible spatial context. (3) DRIVER AGENT stays — the conductor you talk to, now a first-class web-app COMPONENT, not a tmux bottom pane. A term / web view / QR picture are all just content in the main area. Resolves: attention-at-a-glance (=thumbnail strip), no-jump (=downward scroll), driver-as-component (cleaner than Claude-in-a-tmux-pane). Older tmux-layout ideas (standard 3x2, pan-not-flip, edge-scroll, ribbon geometry) are now PROTOTYPE-ERA — reinterpret under this model, don't build on tmux.
